        "result": "Saved `pelican.html` (401 lines, ~18 KB) in the current directory — a single self-contained file with no external assets.\n\n**What's in the animation**\n\n- **The pelican:** body, neck, head, long upper mandible, a throat pouch that inflates and deflates, a little fish still inside it, a blinking eye, a flapping wing, a wagging tail, and a gentle body bob timed to the pedal stroke.\n- **The legs:** solved by two-link inverse kinematics, so the feet actually stay on the pedals through the whole crank revolution (8 keyframes per leg, morphed with SMIL `values`), with a second, darker leg on the far side at 180° phase.\n- **The bicycle:** spoked wheels spinning, chainring and crank turning, chain running as a moving dash pattern, pedals orbiting the bottom bracket while staying flat, frame, fork, saddle, handlebar, bell, and a fluttering pennant.\n- **The world:** gradient sky, pulsing sun, drifting clouds, parallax hills, scrolling trees and grass tufts, and a road whose centre-line dashes slide past.\n- **Controls:** a Pause/Play button and a Slow-motion toggle, using `pauseAnimations()` / `setCurrentRate()` — the page is fully animated without any JavaScript, so the buttons are optional extras.\n\nThe motion is internally consistent: wheel circumference 282.7 px over a 0.8 s revolution gives 353 px/s, and the road dashes, trees, and tufts all cycle at exactly that speed (0.198 s, 0.566 s, 0.283 s). The SVG also parses as well-formed XML, and every path-morph keyframe set has an identical command structure so the interpolation is clean.",
